; ax25_tx.loom
; An AX.25 packet transmitter over Bell 202 AFSK, the stack amateur packet
; radio and APRS use: a UI frame built from the host's callsigns and
; payload, its FCS, HDLC zero-bit insertion and flags, NRZI, and the two
; tones, phase-continuous, at 1200 bit/s. Two threads, one per layer:
; thread 0 is the link layer and leaves an NRZI baseband line on LINE;
; thread 1 is the modem and turns that line into tones on TX.
;
; Written on 2026-11-07, after the hardware was complete (D-032), as a
; protocol Loom was not designed for (D-035). The hardware it uses, and
; what that hardware was built for:
;   - the bit engine's NRZI encoder (SEMANTICS 6.9.1), built for USB, on
;     thread 0: 0 = a transition, which is AX.25's line code too;
;   - the CRC unit (6.9), built for USB, CAN and CRC-8: here the FCS-16,
;     x^16 + x^12 + x^5 + 1, CRC_POLY 0x1021, CRC_INIT 0xFFFF;
;   - LD/ST (6.11), built for the EEPROM and USB programs: the frame buffer;
;   - the PIN_OUT view of the OUT pins (pin indices 16..21, SEMANTICS 3),
;     built so a program can read back what it drives: here thread 1 reads
;     the line thread 0 drives, with no wire and no FIFO between them;
;   - the shared flags (6.5), built for handshakes between threads: they
;     start the two threads' tick grids one clock apart;
;   - the fractional tick and the deadline-latched pin write (6.10), built
;     for UART baud rates and clock-exact edges: the tones.
; What the firmware does that no hardware does: the AX.25 address field
; (each callsign octet shifted left one bit, the extension bit on the
; last), the UI control and PID octets, HDLC's zero-bit insertion (the
; engine's stuffing knows USB's and CAN's rules, not a 0 after five 1s),
; the flags, and the AFSK oscillator itself. The two threads are
; hdlc_loopback.loom's transmitter and bell202_mod.loom's oscillator,
; each changed only where the layers meet.
;
; Why two threads and not one: a thread has eight registers, and the
; oscillator needs four of them every tick while the framer needs all
; eight at a bit boundary; split, each layer keeps its registers, its own
; schedule (one deadline a bit against one a tick) and its own proof, and
; the interface between them is one wire, as it is between a TNC and a
; modem. Nothing has to be interleaved by hand.
;
; The layers meet on LINE. NRZI turns "a 0 is a transition" into a line
; level; 1200 bit/s packet radio sends a 0 as a change of tone and a 1 as
; no change (the TNC-2 / APRS convention; AX.25 2.2 leaves the physical
; layer out, QUESTIONS.md). So the modem
; needs no bit clock and no knowledge of frames: thread 1 sends mark while
; LINE is high and space while it is low, read once a tick. The tone
; changes exactly when the line does, and the line changes only on
; thread 0's bit ticks.
;
; Standards (tools/protomodels/ax25.py gives the sections and marks what
; could not be checked): AX.25 Link Access Protocol, version 2.2 (TAPR,
; July 1998), read 2026-11-07: 3.1 flags (a shared flag allowed), 3.6
; zero-bit insertion, 3.7 FCS per ISO 3309, 3.8 octets LSB first and the
; FCS "most-significant bit first", 3.11 flags as time fill, 3.12 address
; field encoding, 4.3.3.6 UI frame (control 000P0011), 3.4 PID 0xF0 (no
; layer 3), 6.1.2 command/response bits. NRZI and the tones are not in
; AX.25: Bell 202 as bell202_mod.loom cites it (mark 1200 Hz, space
; 2200 Hz, 1200 bit/s, continuous phase).
; Pins: TX = OUT0, the AFSK square wave (an external low-pass makes the
; sine, as for bell202_mod.loom); LINE = OUT1, the NRZI baseband, also on
; its pad (a scope sees the link layer on its own). LINE must be one of
; OUT0..OUT5 (thread 1 reads it through the PIN_OUT view, index 16..21).
;
; Host words, INQ[0] (thread 0):
;   a command: bits 7:0 n, the information octets, 0..240 (the buffer
;     holds 128 words); bits 15:8 ignored;
;   then 7 words, the 14 address octets, two a word, the first in 7:0:
;     the destination callsign (6 ASCII characters, upper case and digits,
;     padded with spaces), its SSID octet, the source callsign and its
;     SSID octet, all before the shift: an SSID octet is given as
;     C << 6 | 0x30 | SSID (the two reserved bits are 1, AX.25 2.2 3.12.2;
;     C is the command/response bit: 1 for the destination of a command
;     frame and 0 for its source, 6.1.2). The program shifts every octet
;     left one bit and sets the extension bit of the last (3.12): that
;     octet's bit 0. Two addresses: no digipeater path (QUESTIONS.md).
;   then (n + 1) / 2 words of information, first octet in 7:0 (an odd last
;     octet in 7:0, 15:8 ignored).
; The program adds control 0x03 (UI, P = 0) and PID 0xF0, computes the FCS
; and sends flag, address, control, PID, information, FCS, flag. Nothing is
; ever pushed to OUTQ.
;
; Timing. Both threads run on one tick, 1/132 of a bit, as bell202_mod.loom
; does: at 50 MHz TICK_INT 315, TICK_FRAC 168 (1.0 ppm fast). The host sets
; it for threads 0 and 1 before RUN, and starts both. Thread 0 waits 132
; ticks a bit; thread 1 one tick. At start thread 1 signals SFLAGS[1],
; thread 0 waits for it and signals SFLAGS[0], and each then rewrites its
; TICK_INT (SEMANTICS 4: ACC <= 0); the order of those slots is fixed, so
; thread 1's tick falls one clock after thread 0's, always. Thread 0's line
; edge is visible by 9 clocks after its tick (a WAITD is up to 3 late, the
; SHO or SETP is the next slot, 2 more for the shared view, SEMANTICS 2);
; thread 1 reads LINE in the fourth slot or later after its own tick, 13
; clocks or more after thread 0's: every bit's tone change falls on the
; same tick of the bit, with no race and no jitter.
; The schedule is proved for a tick of 44 clocks or more (thread 1's
; longest tick is 11 slots). The tones' edges are exact from 46 clocks on:
; a staged SETP lands on its deadline's own edge only if the WAITD after
; it has committed before that tick (SEMANTICS 6.10, rule 1); at 44 the
; WAITD may commit up to 1 clock after it (3 late, 40 more, 2 to commit)
; and the edge falls on the slot grid instead, up to 4 clocks late (seen
; on the model 2026-11-07). At 50 MHz the tick is 315 clocks.
;
; Idle: flags back to back on LINE, so the tones never stop (no carrier
; control: a PTT pin is QUESTIONS.md's). A frame starts two flags after
; its last word is in (an idle flag, then the opening flag); a slow host
; delays a frame but never breaks it.
;
; Memory (.imem 512): 162 words. Thread 0's code is 138 words, more than
; a quarter: 0x000 to 0x046, and on from 0x0A0 to 0x0E2 (the opening flag,
; the FCS and the bit loop), past thread 1's 24 words at 0x080 to 0x097.
; The transmit buffer is thread 2's quarter, 0x100 to 0x17F (128 words,
; written with ST before any LD reads them). ax25_rx.loom (119 words)
; fits beside it in thread 3's quarter. Start threads 0 and 1 only.
;
; Readings taken on the open spec questions (2026-11-07):
;   The physical layer (NRZI, Bell 202 at 1200 baud) is not in AX.25 2.2;
;   it is the TNC-2 / APRS convention, from memory and unchecked, with
;   Bell 202 cited as bell202_mod.loom cites it.
;   FCS bit order: the ISO 3309 (HDLC) order, as the shipped HDLC program
;   sends it; not checked against a frame captured from a real TNC.
;   Two addresses only (no digipeater path), C bits from the host, and the
;   tones run until the threads stop (no PTT or carrier-off pin).
;   AX.25 2.2 figure 3.5 shows 0x98 for a shifted "N"; the text's rule
;   gives 0x9C, which the tests use.

        .imem   512
        .pins   TX = OUT0, LINE = OUT1
        .equ    BIT_T  = 132            ; ticks a bit
        .equ    CFG = (1 << 3) | (1 << 9)       ; ENC NRZI, DIR LSB first, CRC_EN
        .equ    CFGI = CFG | (1 << 7)           ; the same with INV
        .equ    POLY = 0x1021           ; FCS-16, x^16 + x^12 + x^5 + 1
        .equ    FLAG = 0x7E
        .equ    UI = 0x03               ; control: UI frame, P = 0
        .equ    PID = 0xF0              ; no layer 3 protocol
        .equ    TXBUF = 0x100
        .equ    LINEIDX = 17            ; OUT1 in the pin index space (16 + 1)

; --------------------------------------------------------- thread 0: link
; The transmitter of hdlc_loopback.loom, at one bit per 132 ticks: the
; engine sends with ENC = NRZI, STUFF = 0 and CRC_EN, LSB first; after the
; fifth 1 in a row the next bit is a stuff 0 sent by hand (SETP of the
; complement of the line, then BE_CFG rewritten with INV equal to the new
; level, which clears LVL, 6.9.1, so the encoder goes on from the line as
; it stands); the stuff bit never enters SR or the CRC. The FCS: STCRC,
; NOT, REV (the x^15 coefficient first) and sixteen more bits through the
; same loop. Idle flags are raw (never stuffed); while they go out the
; command and its words are taken from INQ, one word a bit, each POP
; guarded by a WAITB INQ_NE, T that completed by condition.
; r0 the command, then the word on the wire   r1 the next raw octet, then
; the 1s still allowed before a stuff bit   r2 the buffer pointer
; r3 words to come, then the CNT reload   r4 bits to go   r5 CFG   r6 CFGI
; r7 scratch, then how many ends are to come (the data's, the FCS's)

        .thread 0
        .tick   44                      ; the fastest tick proved (thread 1 sets it)
        SETP    LINE, 0                 ; the line starts low ...
        .csr    BE_PINS, LINEIDX        ; out = LINE
        .csr    CRC_POLY, POLY
        .csr    CRC_INIT, 0xFFFF
        MOV16   r5, CFG
        MOV16   r6, CFGI
        CSRW    BE_CFG, r5              ; ... and LVL = 0, INV = 0 is the low line
        WAITS   1                       ; thread 1 is at its WAITS 0 ...
        SIG     0                       ; ... and leaves it one clock after us:
        CSRR    r7, TICK_INT            ; both rewrite TICK_INT, so the two
        CSRW    TICK_INT, r7            ; tick grids restart one clock apart
        LDI     r1, FLAG
        SETD    1

; Idle A: flags, and a command taken if one is waiting.
ia:     LDSR    r1                      ; FLAG
        LDI     r4, 8
        MOV16   r2, TXBUF
al:     WAITD   BIT_T
        SHO                             ; raw: idle octets are not stuffed
        WAITB   INQ_NE, T               ; TD has passed: a test, not a wait
        BT      an
        .bounded "guarded by the WAITB INQ_NE above, completed by condition; only thread 0 pops INQ[0]"
        POP     r0                      ; the command, kept for the opening flag
        SHLI    r0, 8
        SHRI    r0, 8                   ; n
        MOV     r3, r0
        ADDI    r3, 15
        SHRI    r3, 1                   ; words to come: 7 + (n + 1) / 2
        LDI     r1, 7                   ; of which address words
        DJNZ    r4, bl
        JMP     ib
an:     DJNZ    r4, al
        JMP     ia

; Idle B: flags, and the frame's words into the buffer, one a bit. The
; seven address words are encoded on the way in (AX.25 2.2, 3.12): both
; octets shifted left one bit (the low octet's bit 7 is 0, ASCII and the
; SSID octet are 7-bit, so it lands as the high octet's bit 0), and the
; last word's bit 7 set first, which is the extension bit E = 1 of the
; last address octet after the shift. Control and PID go in after them.
ib:     LDI     r7, FLAG
        LDSR    r7
        LDI     r4, 8
bl:     WAITD   BIT_T
        SHO
        WAITB   INQ_NE, T
        BT      bn
        .bounded "guarded by the WAITB INQ_NE above, completed by condition; only thread 0 pops INQ[0]"
        POP     r7
        CMPI    r1, 0
        BZ      bs                      ; information: as it is
        DEC     r1                      ; an address word; Z: the last
        BNZ     bsh
        XOR     r7, r7, r5              ; CFG ^ CFGI is INV, bit 7: E,
        XOR     r7, r7, r6              ; once shifted
        SHLI    r7, 1
        ST      r7, r2, 0               ; TXBUF + 6
        INC     r2
        MOV16   r7, (PID << 8) | UI     ; TXBUF + 7: control, PID
        JMP     bs
bsh:    SHLI    r7, 1
bs:     ST      r7, r2, 0
        INC     r2
        DJNZ    r3, bn
        DJNZ    r4, cl                  ; all in: finish this octet, then open
        JMP     open
bn:     DJNZ    r4, bl
        JMP     ib
cl:     WAITD   BIT_T
        SHO
        DJNZ    r4, cl
        JMP     open

; The thread 1 section follows here in memory; the opening flag and the
; FCS set-up are at 0x0A0, after it, in thread 1's quarter.

; -------------------------------------------------------- thread 1: modem
; bell202_mod.loom's oscillator with the bit source changed: no queue, no
; framing, no bit counter. r0 holds the phase still to go in the current
; half-cycle, minus one, in units of 1/396 half-cycle; mark spends 6 a
; tick (396 / 66), space 11 (396 / 36). When a step would pass zero the
; output toggles and r0 gains 396, keeping the remainder, so the phase
; carries across every change of tone. The toggle is staged with SETP ... D
; and lands on the next tick exactly. LINE is read once a tick, in the
; fourth slot or later after the WAITD (see Timing above), and decides the
; step of the ticks that follow: a tone change is one tick after the line
; change on every bit. A bit is 132 ticks of either tone: a mark bit is 792
; units and a space bit 1452, both multiples of 66, so (bell202_mod.loom's
; argument) every crossing of the ideal continuous-phase wave is on a tick.
; r0 phase to go - 1   r1 phase step   r5 HALF   r7 scratch

        .equ    HALF   = 396                    ; phase units a half-cycle
        .equ    MARK   = 6                      ; units a tick at 1200 Hz (396 / 66)
        .equ    SPACE  = 11                     ; units a tick at 2200 Hz (396 / 36)

        .thread 1
        .tick   44                      ; the fastest tick the schedule is proved for
        SETP    TX, 1                   ; a half-cycle starts high
        OEP     TX, 1                   ; drive it (a no-op unless TX is a BIDIR pin)
        MOV16   r5, HALF
        MOV16   r0, HALF - 1            ; a whole half-cycle to go
        LDI     r1, MARK                ; LINE starts low, but idles on flags
        SIG     1                       ; ready ...
        WAITS   0                       ; ... and thread 0 has signalled
        CSRR    r7, TICK_INT
        CSRW    TICK_INT, r7            ; one clock after thread 0's rewrite
        SETD    1

tick:   WAITD   1
        SUB     r0, r0, r1              ; C = 0: this tick ends the half-cycle
        BC      look
        ADD     r0, r0, r5              ; the remainder carries into the next one
        JP      TX, 1, fall
        SETP    TX, 1, D                ; staged: lands on the next tick exactly
        JMP     look
fall:   SETP    TX, 0, D
look:   LDI     r1, MARK                ; high line: mark (LDI keeps the flags)
        JP      LINE, 1, tick
        LDI     r1, SPACE               ; low line: space
        JMP     tick

; ------------------------------------------- thread 0, continued at 0x0A0
; Two more flags, the second the opening flag, with the frame set up
; between their bits. r0 is n.
        .thread 0
        .org    0x0A0
open:   MOV16   r1, (FLAG << 8) | FLAG
        LDSR    r1
        LDI     r1, 14                  ; flag bits 1 to 14 in the loop below
        MOV     r4, r0
        WAITD   BIT_T                   ; flag bit 0
        SHO
        ADDI    r4, 16                  ; the octets: address 14, control, PID, n
        SHLI    r4, 3                   ; the bits
        LDI     r7, 2                   ; ends to come: the data's, the FCS's
        LDI     r3, 16                  ; the CNT reload
        MOV16   r2, TXBUF
        LD      r0, r2, 0               ; the first two address octets
        INC     r2
fo:     WAITD   BIT_T                   ; flag bits 1 to 14
        SHO
        DJNZ    r1, fo
        WAITD   BIT_T                   ; bit 15, the last of the opening flag
        SHO
        CRCI                            ; the FCS covers what follows the flag
        LDSR    r0
        CSRW    CNT, r3
        SHRI    r0, 1                   ; C = the first bit
        LDI     r1, 5                   ; five 1s before a stuff bit
        JMP     txb

; The FCS, entered right after the last data bit's SHO.
fcs:    STCRC   r0
        NOT     r0, r0                  ; sent complemented ...
        REV     r0, r0                  ; ... the x^15 coefficient first
        LDSR    r0
        CSRW    CNT, r3
        LDI     r4, 16
        SHRI    r0, 1                   ; and on into txb

; Every bit of address, control, PID, information and FCS.
txb:    WAITD   BIT_T
        SHO                             ; the bit; Z = (CNT == 0): the word is out
        BC      t1                      ; C = this bit (the SHRI of the copy before)
        LDI     r1, 5                   ; a 0: five more 1s before a stuff bit
tn:     BZ      wend
        SHRI    r0, 1                   ; C = the next bit
        DJNZ    r4, txb                 ; bits left in the data, or in the FCS
        DJNZ    r7, fcs                 ; the data are out: the FCS
close:  LDI     r1, FLAG                ; the FCS is out: the closing flag is
        JMP     ia                      ; the first idle flag
wend:   DJNZ    r4, wl                  ; the word is out: bits left?
        DJNZ    r7, fcs
        JMP     close
wl:     LD      r0, r2, 0               ; the next word
        INC     r2
        LDSR    r0
        CSRW    CNT, r3
        SHRI    r0, 1
        JMP     txb
t1:     DJNZ    r1, tn                  ; a 1: fewer than five so far
        LDI     r1, 5                   ; the fifth: a stuff bit next
        JP      LINE, 1, th             ; the level the stuff bit leaves
        WAITD   BIT_T
        SETP    LINE, 1                 ; the stuff 0: the line rises ...
        CSRW    BE_CFG, r6              ; ... and LVL = 0 is now the high line
ts:     BZ      wend                    ; then on with the bit before it
        SHRI    r0, 1
        DJNZ    r4, txb
        DJNZ    r7, fcs
        JMP     close
th:     WAITD   BIT_T
        SETP    LINE, 0                 ; the line falls ...
        CSRW    BE_CFG, r5              ; ... and LVL = 0 is the low line
        JMP     ts

